A decreasing array is a max heap, so another simple option is to repeatedly place the largest remaining value at the next position. This is slower than heapify, but the result is very clear and easy to verify.

The method rearranges `nums` itself. If the array has no elements or just one element, there is nothing to do.

```csharp
class Solution {
    public int[] MinToMaxHeap(int[] nums) {
        for (int position = 0; position < nums.Length; position++) {
            int largestIndex = position;

            for (int i = position + 1; i < nums.Length; i++) {
                if (nums[i] > nums[largestIndex]) {
                    largestIndex = i;
                }
            }

            int temp = nums[position];
            nums[position] = nums[largestIndex];
            nums[largestIndex] = temp;
        }

        return nums;
    }
}
```

The important edge cases are an empty array, a single value, and repeated values. All of them are safe with the loops above.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because each position scans the remaining values.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

The array already has the shape of a complete binary tree, so only the parent values need to be fixed. Starting from the last parent, sift each value down and keep the larger child above it.

For example, `[1, 3, 2, 4]` becomes `[4, 3, 2, 1]` after the parent nodes are repaired. The function works on the copied C++ parameter, so the returned vector is the converted heap.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> minToMaxHeap(vector<int> nums) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(nums.size());
        if (n <= 1) {
            return nums;
        }

        for (int parent = n / 2 - 1; parent >= 0; --parent) {
            int current = parent;

            while (true) {
                int left = 2 * current + 1;
                int right = left + 1;
                int largest = current;

                if (left < n && nums[left] > nums[largest]) {
                    largest = left;
                }
                if (right < n && nums[right] > nums[largest]) {
                    largest = right;
                }

                if (largest == current) {
                    break;
                }

                swap(nums[current], nums[largest]);
                current = largest;
            }
        }

        return nums;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the copied parameter. The empty array is returned unchanged.

The tree can be fixed from the bottom, but the downward movement is written recursively here. For each parent, the larger child is selected and the same check continues below it after a swap.

The function copies the input slice before changing it. That keeps the caller's slice unchanged and returns a separate max-heap slice.

```go
func minToMaxHeap(originalNums []int) []int {
    nums := append([]int(nil), originalNums...)
    n := len(nums)

    var siftDown func(int)
    siftDown = func(index int) {
        left := 2*index + 1
        right := left + 1
        largest := index

        if left < n && nums[left] > nums[largest] {
            largest = left
        }
        if right < n && nums[right] > nums[largest] {
            largest = right
        }

        if largest == index {
            return
        }

        nums[index], nums[largest] = nums[largest], nums[index]
        siftDown(largest)
    }

    for parent := n/2 - 1; parent >= 0; parent-- {
        siftDown(parent)
    }

    return nums
}
```

For `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, the lower parent is fixed first, then the root is fixed using the new child values. The result is a valid max heap such as `[4, 2, 3, 1]`.

Time complexity: `O(n)` overall.

Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursive call stack, plus the copied slice of `O(n)`.

A sorted array in decreasing order is always a valid max heap, because every parent appears before all of its children and is at least as large. This makes the conversion easy to check, although it does more work than bottom-up heap construction.

The input array is changed directly. Sorting also handles duplicate values without any special case.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int[] minToMaxHeap(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);

        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            int temp = nums[left];
            nums[left] = nums[right];
            nums[right] = temp;
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return nums;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for sorting.

Space complexity: `O(log n)` extra space used by the primitive array sort. An empty or one-element array is already a max heap.
